Getting fiddly. Let me simplify using URI parsing:
```java
private boolean isEntryAuthority(String authority, boolean allowDefaultPort) {
    if (authority == null || authority.isBlank()) return false;
    String host;
    String portText;
    if (authority.startsWith("[")) {
        int end = authority.indexOf(']');
        if (end < 0) return false;
        host = authority.substring(0, end + 1);
        portText = authority.length() > end + 1 && authority.charAt(end + 1) == ':'
                ? authority.substring(end + 2) : "";
    } else {
        int colon = authority.lastIndexOf(':');
        host = colon < 0 ? authority : authority.substring(0, colon);
        portText = colon < 0 ? "" : authority.substring(colon + 1);
    }
    if (!LOOPBACK_HOSTS.contains(host.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT))) return false;
    return portText.isEmpty() || portText.equals(Integer.toString(port));
}
```

`isEntryOrigin(originHeader)`: parse with `URI.create`; scheme must be `http`; then `isEntryAuthority(uri.getRawAuthority(), false)`. Also require port explicitly? `Origin: http://127.0.0.1:PORT`. If port missing, default 80 ≠ ours → reject unless our port is 80 (unlikely). `isEntryAuthority` with empty port returns true; that would allow `Origin: http://localhost` which defaults to port 80 — a cross-origin request from a server on port 80. Extremely unlikely and not a real threat (an attacker's page on port 80 of 127.0.0.1? that's a local malicious server, which is already game over). Let me require explicit port match in Origin: `portText.equals(...)`. Let me add a flag.

Simpler: for Origin, require the authority to contain an explicit port equal to ours. For Host, the browser always sends the port for non-default ports; our port is never 80/443 in practice, so requiring an explicit port for Host too would be fine... but if someone configures port 80 the Host header would be `localhost`. Edge case. Let me allow empty port for Host and require explicit for Origin. Slight asymmetry; document briefly in code.

Hmm, actually for DNS rebinding protection, the Host check is the critical one and it must be strict. `Host: evil.com` → host not in loopback set → rejected. Good. Empty port just means default 80; if our port isn't 80 the connection wouldn't have reached us with that Host unless... Actually a rebinding attack: attacker's DNS name resolves to 127.0.0.1, browser sends `Host: evil.com:PORT`. So host = "evil.com" → rejected. If attacker uses port 80 and we listen on 80... we don't. So allow-empty-port for Host is safe.

Let me also require `write` to have `Origin` present. Note: does the browser send `Origin` on same-origin POST? Yes — per Fetch spec, POST requests include Origin. Chrome/Firefox/Safari do. Good.

But careful: `curl`-style tests / the Playwright/dev tooling would need to send Origin. Our own REST e2e tests will. Also `verify-stage5-real-topology.mjs` might POST to the API without Origin — but only for the orchestration route, which those scripts don't call. Good.

